6. README.md
Mini Device Fleet Monitor
A small HTTP service that monitors a fleet of simulated devices. Devices sendheartbeats; the service keeps the latest heartbeat per device and exposes thefleet's live status. A device is ONLINE if it has sent a heartbeat within thelast 30 seconds, otherwise OFFLINE. Status is derived on every read, so itis always current — no background timer needed.

Quick start
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activatepip install -r requirements.txtpython -m fleet_monitor            # API on http://localhost:8000# second terminalpython simulator/simulate.py       # 5 devices, heartbeat every 5s, interactive# or: python simulator/simulate.py --demo   (~75s scripted stop/restart demo)# testspytest -v
Design / architecture
                 ┌──────────────────────────────────┐ devices / sim → │ HTTP layer (main.py, FastAPI)    │                 │  - request validation (schemas)  │                 │  - domain errors → 404 / 409     │                 ├──────────────────────────────────┤                 │ Store (store.py)                 │                 │  - dict of DeviceRecord + Lock   │                 │  - status derived on read        │                 │  - injectable clock              │                 └──────────────────────────────────┘
Key decisions:

Status is computed, not stored. Every endpoint derives ONLINE/OFFLINE fromnow - last_seen. This removes any drift between "what the store thinks" and"what the API reports", needs no background sweeper, and makes behaviour easyto test.
Timeout uses server receive time, not the client timestamp. Client clockscan be skewed; using client timestamps would let a device look ONLINE forever(future timestamp) or instantly OFFLINE (slow clock). The client-reportedtimestamp is still validated, stored, and echoed back as last_heartbeat forobservability, matching the spec's response example.
Injectable clock. Store(clock=...) accepts any callable returning a UTCdatetime. Tests inject a fake clock and verify the timeout boundary exactly,without sleeping.
Thread-safe store. Route handlers are synchronous, so FastAPI serves themfrom a thread pool; a threading.Lock protects the device map (covered by aconcurrency test).
Thin HTTP layer. Validation lives in pydantic schemas; domain errors(DuplicateDeviceError, UnknownDeviceError) are mapped to 409/404 byexception handlers.
Endpoints
Method	Path	Description
POST	/devices	Register a device (201, 409 on dup)
POST	/devices/{id}/heartbeat	Record a heartbeat (200, 404 if unknown)
GET	/devices?status=ONLINE	List devices, optional status filter
GET	/devices/{id}	Device details (404 if unknown)
GET	/summary	{total, online, offline}
GET	/health	Liveness probe
Prerequisites
Python 3.10+
No external services; state is in-memory.
Configuration (environment variables)
Variable	Default	Meaning
HOST	0.0.0.0	Bind address
PORT	8000	Listen port
HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS	30	ONLINE/OFFLINE threshold
Running
python -m fleet_monitor                # or: uvicorn fleet_monitor.main:app --reload
Or with Docker:

docker build -t fleet-monitor .docker run -p 8000:8000 fleet-monitor
Note: state is in-memory per process — run with a single worker.

Simulator
python simulator/simulate.py                 # interactivepython simulator/simulate.py --demo          # scripted verification (~75s)python simulator/simulate.py --interval 2    # faster heartbeats
Interactive commands: stop device-03, start device-03, status, quit.The --demo mode pauses device-03, prints /summary every 5s until it showsOFFLINE, then resumes it and shows it return to ONLINE.

Tests
pytest -v
Covers: registration (incl. duplicates/invalid ids), heartbeat handling(incl. missing/invalid fields), the 30s ONLINE/OFFLINE boundary (exactly 30s =ONLINE, 30.001s = OFFLINE), recovery after a stale period, summary counts,status filtering, and concurrent heartbeats from 8 threads.

Example API requests
 $ curl -s -X POST localhost:8000/devices -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \    -d '{"id":"device-01","name":"Lab Device 01"}'{"id":"device-01","name":"Lab Device 01","status":"OFFLINE","last_heartbeat":null,...} $ curl -s -X POST localhost:8000/devices/device-01/heartbeat -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \    -d '{"timestamp":"2026-09-21T10:30:00Z","status":"OK","cpu_usage":42,"signal_strength":-71}'{"id":"device-01","status":"ONLINE","last_heartbeat":"2026-09-21T10:30:00.000000Z",...,"heartbeat_count":1} $ curl -s localhost:8000/summary{"total":1,"online":1,"offline":0} $ curl -s -X POST localhost:8000/devices/device-01/heartbeat -d '{}'{"detail":[{"type":"missing","loc":["body","status"],...}]}      # 422 $ curl -s localhost:8000/devices/ghost{"detail":"unknown device 'ghost'"}                              # 404
Assumptions
Duplicate registration returns 409 (no update-in-place).
Heartbeats from unregistered devices return 404 (no auto-registration).
A registered device that never sent a heartbeat is OFFLINE.
Exactly 30s since the last heartbeat is still ONLINE ("within the last 30seconds"); more than 30s is OFFLINE.
timestamp is optional; if omitted, the server uses its receive time. Naivetimestamps are assumed UTC.
status (e.g. "OK") is an opaque device-reported string, validated fornon-emptiness only; ONLINE/OFFLINE is derived purely from recency.
Unknown extra fields in a heartbeat are ignored.
Known limitations
In-memory, single process: a restart loses state; multiple workers would eachhave their own state.
Only the latest heartbeat is kept; no history.
No authentication, pagination, or rate limiting.
Wall-clock based; large NTP jumps could momentarily affect statuses.
With one more day
Persistence (SQLite via a storage interface) + a background sweeper only forside effects like alerts.
SSE/WebSocket live fleet updates and a minimal dashboard.
Structured JSON logging with request ids; Prometheus metrics.
CI (GitHub Actions: pytest + lint), docker-compose for API + simulator.
Auth (API keys), pagination, and fuzz/property tests for the store.
