README.md
Copy-paste this entire block:

Mini Device Fleet Monitor
A small HTTP service that monitors a fleet of simulated devices. Devices sendheartbeats; the service keeps the latest heartbeat per device and exposes thefleet's live status. A device is ONLINE if it has sent a heartbeat within thelast 30 seconds, otherwise OFFLINE. Status is derived on every read, so itis always current — no background timer needed.

Quick start
python -m venv .venv.venv\Scripts\activate          # Windows (Linux/Mac: source .venv/bin/activate)pip install -r requirements.txtpython -m fleet_monitor         # terminal 1: API on http://127.0.0.1:8000python simulator/simulate.py --demo   # terminal 2: 5 devices + scripted stop/restart demo (~75s)python -m pytest -v             # run the test suite (26 tests)
Design / architecture
                  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐ devices / sim →  │ HTTP layer (main.py, FastAPI)    │                  │  - request validation (schemas)  │                  │  - domain errors → 404 / 409     │                  ├──────────────────────────────────┤                  │ Store (store.py)                 │                  │  - dict of DeviceRecord + Lock   │                  │  - status derived on read        │                  │  - injectable clock              │                  └──────────────────────────────────┘
Key decisions:

Status is computed, not stored. Every endpoint derives ONLINE/OFFLINE fromnow - last_seen. This removes any drift between "what the store thinks" and"what the API reports", needs no background sweeper, and makes behaviour easyto test.
Timeout uses server receive time, not the client timestamp. Client clockscan be skewed; using client timestamps would let a device look ONLINE forever(future timestamp) or instantly OFFLINE (slow clock). The client-reportedtimestamp is still validated, stored, and echoed back as last_heartbeat,matching the spec's response example.
Injectable clock. Store(clock=...) accepts any callable returning a UTCdatetime. Tests inject a fake clock and verify the timeout boundary exactly,without sleeping.
Thread-safe store. Route handlers are synchronous, so FastAPI serves themfrom a thread pool; a threading.Lock protects the device map (covered by aconcurrency test).
Thin HTTP layer. Validation lives in pydantic schemas; domain errors(DuplicateDeviceError, UnknownDeviceError) are mapped to 409/404 byexception handlers.
Endpoints
Method	Path	Description
POST	/devices	Register a device (201, 409 on duplicate)
POST	/devices/{id}/heartbeat	Record a heartbeat (200, 404 if unknown)
GET	/devices?status=ONLINE	List devices, optional status filter
GET	/devices/{id}	Device details (404 if unknown)
GET	/summary	{total, online, offline}
GET	/health	Liveness probe
Interactive API docs are auto-generated at /docs while the server runs.

Prerequisites
Python 3.10+
No external services; state is in-memory.
Configuration (environment variables)
Variable	Default	Meaning
HOST	0.0.0.0	Bind address
PORT	8000	Listen port
HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS	30	ONLINE/OFFLINE threshold
Running
python -m fleet_monitor# or: uvicorn fleet_monitor.main:app --reload
Or with Docker:

docker build -t fleet-monitor .docker run -p 8000:8000 fleet-monitor
Note: state is in-memory per process — run with a single worker.

