Thought Process
Good observation — and let me first make sure I understand: you stopped a device (or the whole simulator), it went OFFLINE, and when it came back the heartbeat count continued (e.g. hb# 8, 9, 10...) instead of going back to 0. Correct?

If so: that's not a bug — it's the correct behavior, and understanding why is genuinely interview-level insight. Let me walk through it.

The one-sentence answer
Nothing resets because the data lives in the server's memory — and only restarting the server wipes it. Stopping a device, stopping the simulator, even closing the dashboard tab — none of those touch the server's state.

What you stop
What happens to the data
stop device-03 (simulator)	Record stays; count keeps its value; resumes counting from there
Whole simulator (Ctrl+C)	All records stay, all devices go OFFLINE after 30s, counts frozen
Dashboard browser tab	Nothing at all — the UI is just a window; reopening re-fetches the same state
The server (Ctrl+C on terminal 1)	Everything resets — in-memory dict is gone. Counts 0, devices gone

The UI displays data; the simulator sends data; only the server owns data.

Why a resumed device shouldn't reset its count
Look at what your record actually contains when device-03 comes back:

status: ONLINE ← derived, computed fresh on every read from now − last_seen
heartbeat_count: 8 ← stored, a lifetime fact: "this device has sent 8 heartbeats in its life"
Two different kinds of data:

Derived data (status, seconds-since) "resets" naturally by the passage of time — nothing to clear.
Stored data (count, last metrics, last heartbeat time) is history, and history surviving an outage is the point. When device-03 was OFFLINE, your dashboard could still show "OFFLINE, last heard from 11:41:20, last CPU 63%" — that's exactly the evidence an operator needs to diagnose a dead device. Wiping it on offline would delete the clues.
And think about what a reset-to-zero counter would mean: any flaky device with a bad Wi-Fi connection would reset its counter every time it blipped. The number would measure "time since last blip," not "how alive is this device." Real monitoring systems work exactly this way — Prometheus, for example, has a rule that counters only ever go up; you compute activity rates from them.

The deep reason (this is the interview gold)
Remember your core design: status is derived on read, there's no background thread.

That means "going OFFLINE is not an event in your system." At t+30s, no code runs. Nobody flips a switch, nobody fires a hook, nothing observes the transition. The device doesn't "become" offline — it just is offline the next time someone asks.

So there is literally no place in the code where a reset could happen, because there's no moment of "becoming offline." The only detectable transition is the reverse: the next heartbeat arriving after a stale period. That's the only place you could implement "reset on recovery" — and that tells you something important about the trade-off:

Derived-on-read bought you simplicity and perfect testability. The price is that you don't get "offline events" for free — if you ever need them (for alerting, for session resets), you either add transition detection on the next heartbeat, or add a background sweeper and accept its complexity.

Being able to say that sentence in an interview is worth more than the feature itself.

